    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d420">

                <pb facs="0440" n="436" sameAs="frus:frus1939v03/pg436"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.515/1404: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Chargé in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Dooman</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-07-31T20:00:00-04:00">July 31, 1939—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>233. Reference Department’s 201, July 15, 5 p.m.<anchor n="91" xml:id="d420-f1"/>
                </p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>A reply has been received, in the form of an <hi rendition="#italic"
                            >aide-mémoire</hi> from the British Embassy, to the Department’s
                        communication summarized in the telegram under reference. The Department
                        assumes that your <pb facs="0441" n="437" xml:id="pg437"/> British colleague
                        will have been informed by his Government of the contents of this <hi
                            rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi>, and assumes that he will have
                        informed or upon request will inform you fully of its contents.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>The Department desires that you inform the British Ambassador of what you
                        have already done toward impressing the Japanese Government with the
                        interest and concern of this Government in regard to the broader aspects of
                        the situation at Tientsin, including the currency aspect (as reported in
                        your telegrams 284, June 19, 8 p.m.,<anchor n="92" xml:id="d420-f2"/> and
                        354, July 23, noon<anchor n="93" xml:id="d420-f3"/>), and that you continue
                        your efforts along this line. Although we do not share in every particular
                        the British Government’s views regarding the implications of the facts in
                        the situation and regarding probable consequences of possible courses of
                        action, we desire that you emphasize in your conversations with Japanese
                        authorities the fact that the question of currency developments in China is
                        necessarily a question of concern to the United States and that adverse
                        effects upon American interests of action taken by the Japanese in
                        connection therewith must inevitably adversely affect public and
                        governmental opinion in this country.</item>
                </list>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
